Bootstrap: docker
From: quay.io/biocontainers/canu:2.3--h3fb4750_0

%labels
    Maintainer sdweave2@ncsu.edu
    Source https://github.com/marbl/canu
    Version 2.3

%help
    canu — single-molecule sequencing assembler for high-noise long reads
    (PacBio CLR/HiFi, Oxford Nanopore).

    THIS IS A LOCALLY-PATCHED IMAGE, not the vanilla biocontainer. It is
    the stock quay.io/biocontainers/canu:2.3--h3fb4750_0 image (the same
    image the canu/2.3 module originally shipped) with exactly one file
    added: /usr/bin/command (plus the empty Hazel bind-mount dirs).
    Canu itself, its Perl code, Java, and every other tool in the image
    are unchanged from the upstream biocontainer.

    Replaced the stock image (quay.io_biocontainers_canu:2.3--h3fb4750_0.sif)
    as the canu/2.3 module on 2026-11-16, deployed as canu-2.3.sif; the
    module name/version did not change. The stock image is backed up in
    custom_container_module/tools/canu/pre_deploy_backup_20261116/.
    Re-pulling the plain biocontainer tag will NOT carry this fix.

    The bug: canu's checkJava() (lib/perl5/site_perl/canu/Defaults.pm,
    ~line 1078) resolves java with a Perl backtick:
        $java = `command -v $java`;
    That string has no shell metacharacters, so Perl skips /bin/sh and
    execs the first word directly as a program. `command` is a POSIX
    shell builtin, not a program — it only works where the OS ships a
    /usr/bin/command helper (RHEL/Rocky do; this Debian 12 biocontainer
    does not). In the stock image this fails with:
        Can't exec "command": No such file or directory at .../Defaults.pm line 1078.
        readline() on closed filehandle F at .../Defaults.pm line 1096.
        ERROR:  mhap overlapper requires java version at least 1.8.0; you have unknown (from '').
    i.e. canu never finds java at all, even though OpenJDK 23 is present
    at /usr/local/bin/java. Any run that uses the MHAP overlapper (the
    default for -nanopore read correction) dies at configure time.
    Passing java=<path> does not help: the path still goes through the
    same `command -v` backtick first.

    The fix: add /usr/bin/command as a two-line /bin/sh script that calls
    the builtin (`command "$@"`). Inside sh, `command` resolves to the
    builtin before PATH, so the script cannot recurse. This is the same
    helper RHEL ships for the same reason. Baked into the image (rather
    than bind-mounted by the module) so it also works for users who run
    the .sif directly with `apptainer exec`.

    Workaround on the stock image: corOverlapper=minimap skips
    checkJava() entirely for -nanopore runs (changes the read-correction
    overlapper from the default MHAP).

    Not yet reported upstream (marbl/canu) — the real fix there is to
    make that backtick go through a shell.

    No %runscript on purpose (deviates from this repo's usual
    `exec <tool> "$@"` convention): this image must stay functionally
    identical to the stock image it replaced in place, so it keeps the
    biocontainer's own OCI runscript, which Apptainer generates from the
    docker base when %runscript is absent. `apptainer run <img> <cmd>`
    therefore runs <cmd> via /usr/local/env-execute, and a bare
    `apptainer run <img>` opens /bin/sh, exactly as before. The canu/2.3
    module wrapper uses `exec canu` and is unaffected either way.

    Usage:
        apptainer exec canu-2.3.sif canu [options] -p <prefix> -d <dir> genomeSize=<n> -nanopore <reads.fq>

    Example:
        apptainer exec canu-2.3.sif canu -p asm -d asm_out genomeSize=116m \
            minReadLength=10000 useGrid=false -nanopore reads.fq.gz

    See https://canu.readthedocs.io/en/latest/ for full documentation.

%post -c /bin/bash
    set -e
    set -o pipefail

    # Hazel bind-mount points
    mkdir -p /rs1 /share /home /usr/local/usrapps

    # BRC fix: give Perl's no-shell backtick exec path a real `command`
    # program to find (see %help). Lives in /usr/bin to match where RHEL
    # ships the same helper; /bin is a symlink to usr/bin on this
    # usr-merged Debian 12 base, so /bin/command resolves too.
    cat > /usr/bin/command << 'CMDEOF'
#!/bin/sh
# BRC shim: lets programs that exec `command` directly (e.g. Perl
# backticks with no shell metacharacters) reach the POSIX sh builtin.
# Inside sh, `command` is the builtin, so this does not recurse.
command "$@"
CMDEOF
    chmod 755 /usr/bin/command

    # Fail the build here, not at a user's runtime, if the shim doesn't
    # resolve java the way canu's checkJava() calls it.
    perl -e 'my $j = `command -v java`; $j =~ s/\s+$//; exit($j =~ m{/java$} ? 0 : 1)'

%test
    # Exercises the real failing code path, not just `canu -version`
    # (which passes on the broken stock image too):
    # 1. the exact Perl backtick form canu uses must resolve java;
    perl -e 'my $j = `command -v java`; $j =~ s/\s+$//; exit($j =~ m{/java$} ? 0 : 1)'
    # 2. canu's own configure step must detect java. The run is expected
    #    to exit nonzero afterwards (tiny dummy input / build-host resource
    #    limits), so check the configure output, not the exit code.
    #
    # Do NOT trust an inherited TMPDIR here: apptainer build runs %test
    # with host environment variables (including TMPDIR) passed through,
    # but the build-time root filesystem is read-only and only has the
    # directories %post explicitly created (/rs1 /share /home
    # /usr/local/usrapps) -- not a full mirror of the host's /share tree.
    # A host TMPDIR like /share/brc/<user>/tmp doesn't exist inside the
    # image at build time, so mktemp silently fails, $tmp ends up empty,
    # and writes meant for "$tmp/tiny.fq" land on "/" instead, which is
    # read-only. Force a known-writable location and check mktemp's exit
    # status instead of assuming it succeeded.
    unset TMPDIR
    tmp=$(mktemp -d /tmp/canu_test.XXXXXX) || { echo "ERROR: mktemp failed to create a test directory under /tmp" >&2; exit 1; }
    printf '@r1\nACGTACGTACGTACGTACGT\n+\nIIIIIIIIIIIIIIIIIIII\n' > "$tmp/tiny.fq"
    canu -p t -d "$tmp/out" genomeSize=1m useGrid=false -nanopore "$tmp/tiny.fq" > "$tmp/canu.log" 2>&1 || true
    status=0
    grep -q -- "-- Detected Java" "$tmp/canu.log" || status=1
    grep -q "Failed Java version check" "$tmp/canu.log" && status=1
    [ "$status" -eq 0 ] || cat "$tmp/canu.log"
    rm -rf "$tmp"
    exit "$status"
